WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.258
Welcome to Module Two: Physical Layer Management.

2
00:00:03.588 --> 00:00:07.441
It covers two pieces of hardware you configure by hand:

3
00:00:07.491 --> 00:00:10.279
BlueField DPUs and MIG-partitioned GPUs.

4
00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:15.488
Picture an AI cloud provider filling a new rack of H100 servers.

5
00:00:15.818 --> 00:00:17.795
Within a month, two tenants are unhappy.

6
00:00:18.595 --> 00:00:21.899
The first is an inference team serving small models,

7
00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:24.027
and each one claims a whole H100.

8
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:28.051
Most of every GPU sits idle, and when they try sharing,

9
00:00:28.101 --> 00:00:30.366
one noisy job slows everyone down.

10
00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:35.383
The second tenant is storage-heavy, streaming data over the network all day.

11
00:00:35.713 --> 00:00:39.021
Their host CPUs drown in packet processing, encryption,

12
00:00:39.071 --> 00:00:42.319
and storage traffic, leaving little for the real work.

13
00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:46.973
For the first tenant, the answer is MIG: Multi-Instance GPU.

14
00:00:47.303 --> 00:00:50.520
One H100 is sliced into up to seven instances,

15
00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.717
each with its own memory, cache, and compute.

16
00:00:54.517 --> 00:00:56.829
The isolation is enforced in hardware,

17
00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:00.931
so a noisy neighbour can't steal another slice's memory bandwidth.

18
00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:05.840
Seven small models now share one GPU, each with predictable latency.

19
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.970
For the second tenant, the answer is a BlueField DPU:

20
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.393
a network card with its own Arm cores.

21
00:01:12.723 --> 00:01:17.153
It takes over networking, storage, and security work that used to run on the host.

22
00:01:17.953 --> 00:01:21.487
The host CPUs get their cycles back for the application.

23
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:25.564
And the infrastructure now runs isolated from the tenant's own code.

24
00:01:26.364 --> 00:01:29.109
Lesson one configures the BlueField platform.

25
00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:33.285
You'll install a BFB image over rshim or through the BMC,

26
00:01:33.335 --> 00:01:35.405
then confirm it booted cleanly.

27
00:01:36.205 --> 00:01:40.443
You'll switch between DPU mode and NIC mode, and know what each one gives you.

28
00:01:40.773 --> 00:01:41.913
And you'll meet DOCA,

29
00:01:41.963 --> 00:01:45.992
the software framework for offloading networking, storage, and security.

30
00:01:46.792 --> 00:01:49.793
Lesson two configures MIG for AI and HPC.

31
00:01:50.123 --> 00:01:52.935
You'll enable MIG mode with nvidia-smi,

32
00:01:52.985 --> 00:01:57.411
create GPU instances, and then compute instances inside them.

33
00:01:58.211 --> 00:02:00.810
You'll read profile names like 1g.10gb,

34
00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.071
which spell out each slice's compute and memory.

35
00:02:04.401 --> 00:02:08.660
And you'll learn when MIG fits: many small jobs, not one giant training run.

36
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.265
This domain is five percent of the exam: small,

37
00:02:12.315 --> 00:02:14.999
but the marks are concrete and command-level.

38
00:02:15.329 --> 00:02:18.053
Lesson one starts with the BlueField DPU.
